.TH "MAKNOON" "1" "Jan 2024" "Maknoon Post-Quantum Engine" "Maknoon Manual"

.SH NAME
maknoon-init - Guided first-run setup — create identity, vault, and verify your environment


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmaknoon init [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Walks through all necessary steps to get started with Maknoon:
.IP "  1." 5
Create ~/.maknoon/ directory structure
.IP "  2." 5
Generate a default ML-KEM-768 + ML-DSA-87 identity
.IP "  3." 5
Initialise a default encrypted vault
.IP "  4." 5
Optionally enable audit logging
.IP "  5." 5
Smoke-test encrypt → decrypt to confirm everything works

.PP
Use --non-interactive for Docker/CI environments (all defaults applied, no prompts).


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for init

.PP
\fB--non-interactive\fP[=false]
	Skip all prompts and apply defaults (suitable for Docker/CI)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--fips\fP[=false]
	Enforce strict FIPS-140 compliant cryptographic standards

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Output results in JSON format

.PP
\fB--passphrase-fd\fP=0
	Read passphrase from file descriptor N (e.g., --passphrase-fd 3  3<<<$(pass show key)). Also settable via MAKNOON_PASSPHRASE_FD

.PP
\fB--passphrase-file\fP=""
	Read passphrase from a file instead of prompting (use - for stdin). Also settable via MAKNOON_PASSPHRASE_FILE

.PP
\fB--policy\fP=""
	Path to a declarative governance policy file (JSON)

.PP
\fB--tpm\fP[=false]
	Enable TPM 2.0 hardware-backed storage for keys

.PP
\fB--tpm-device\fP="/dev/tpmrm0"
	Path to the TPM device node

.PP
\fB--tpm-pcrs\fP=[]
	List of PCRs to bind keys to (e.g., 7,14)

.PP
\fB--trace\fP[=false]
	Enable low-level diagnostic tracing to stderr


